  "prompt": "SQLite: LIMIT -1 (no limit) is transpiled verbatim, other dialects reject it\n\nIn SQLite a negative `LIMIT` means \"no limit\", and it's the usual way to write an `OFFSET` without a limit. sqlglot parses it as a plain `-1`, so every other target gets an invalid query:\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\n\nsql = \"SELECT x FROM t LIMIT -1 OFFSET 5\"\nsqlglot.transpile(sql, read=\"sqlite\", write=\"duckdb\")\n# ['SELECT x FROM t LIMIT -1 OFFSET 5']  -> DuckDB: \"LIMIT/OFFSET cannot be negative\"\nsqlglot.transpile(sql, read=\"sqlite\", write=\"postgres\")\n# ['SELECT x FROM t LIMIT -1 OFFSET 5']  -> ERROR: LIMIT must not be negative\nsqlglot.transpile(sql, read=\"sqlite\", write=\"tsql\")\n# ['SELECT x FROM t ORDER BY (SELECT NULL) OFFSET 5 ROWS FETCH FIRST -1 ROWS ONLY']\n```\n\nExpected: the same output as reading `LIMIT ALL OFFSET 5` from Postgres, e.g. `LIMIT ALL OFFSET 5` for DuckDB and `OFFSET 5 ROWS` for T-SQL.\n\nThe SQLite generator already goes the other way: `SELECT x FROM t OFFSET 5` from Postgres is written as `LIMIT -1 OFFSET 5` for SQLite, so a postgres -> sqlite -> duckdb round trip currently breaks.\n\nPossible fix: in the SQLite parser, treat a negative numeric `LIMIT` literal as `LIMIT ALL`.\n\nTested on main (`b4babf3a`).\n\n**Official Documentation**\nhttps://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html#limitoffset (\"If the LIMIT expression evaluates to a negative value, then there is no upper bound on the number of rows returned.\")",
